                s("Risk vs. Issue — Critical Distinction", tableHeaders = listOf("Risk", "Issue"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Definition", "Uncertain event that MIGHT happen in the future", "Problem that HAS ALREADY occurred or is occurring now"), listOf("Keywords in question", "May, might, could potentially, if, possibility of", "Will, should, has happened, is happening, occurred"), listOf("First Action", "Add to Risk Register → Analyze → Plan Response", "Update Issue Log immediately"), listOf("Where documented", "Risk Register (NOT risk management plan)", "Issue Log"), listOf("After resolution", "N/A — ongoing monitoring", "Update Lessons Learned + OPA"))),
                s("Risk vs. Issue — Critical Distinction", "Remember: Risks are POTENTIAL — they go in the Risk Register. Issues are REAL and NOW — they go in the Issue Log. Do NOT confuse these two documents.", SectionKind.KEY),
                s("Conflict Resolution — PMI Priority Order", tableHeaders = listOf("Rank", "Technique", "Description", "PMI View"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("1 ✅", "Collaborate / Problem Solve", "All parties work toward a win-win solution; addresses root cause", "ALWAYS the preferred answer"), listOf("2", "Compromise / Reconcile", "Each party gives something; partially satisfies both", "Good when full collaboration is not possible"), listOf("3", "Smooth / Accommodate", "Downplay differences; emphasise areas of agreement", "Temporary — does not fix root cause"), listOf("4", "Force / Direct", "One party's position wins; win-lose outcome", "Only in emergencies or time-critical situations"), listOf("5 ❌", "Withdraw / Avoid", "Ignore or postpone the conflict entirely", "Almost always wrong — never a long-term solution"))),
                s("Conflict Resolution — PMI Priority Order", "For conflict between two team members: meet with each party INDIVIDUALLY first, then bring them together. Face-to-face communication is always the preferred method.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Keyword-to-Action Map", tableHeaders = listOf("Keyword in Question", "PM Action"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Do / Do First / Do Next", "Assess impact → evaluate scope/time/cost/quality → involve stakeholders → take measured action"), listOf("Have Done / Should Have Done", "Proactive measures: detailed planning, risk process, regular stakeholder communication"), listOf("Not Do / Not Have Done", "Avoid: ignoring feedback, disregarding risks, scope creep, bypassing change control"), listOf("Never Do", "Never: fire/hire mid-project, terminate contracts without analysis, implement changes without CCB, ignore safety or ethics"), listOf("Least Likely / Most Likely", "Order from Most → Least important. Most Likely = highest priority action in the scenario"), listOf("Safety issue arises", "STOP all work immediately. Address safety first, then follow change control processes"), listOf("New law / regulation", "Update Risk Register → adjust project plans → ensure compliance is achieved"), listOf("Project closure", "Formal acceptance → Lessons Learned → Transition to Operations → Disband team"), listOf("Estimation needed", "The TEAM estimates — they have subject matter expertise (SME). PM does not estimate for them."), listOf("When to escalate?", "Only when issue is beyond PM authority AND internal resolution has been genuinely attempted"))),
                s("Traditional PM vs. Agile Scrum Master Mindset", tableHeaders = listOf("Aspect", "Traditional PM", "Agile Scrum Master"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Team Leadership", "Directive; PM assigns tasks and controls work", "Servant leader; team self-organises and self-assigns"), listOf("Planning Approach", "Comprehensive upfront documentation required", "Adaptive; just enough; rolling wave; sprint-by-sprint"), listOf("Change Management", "Formal change requests through CCB; baselines protected", "PO reprioritises backlog; lightweight process; embraces change"), listOf("Stakeholder Engagement", "Identify and analyze at start; manage throughout", "Continuous collaboration; PO as primary stakeholder interface"), listOf("Handling Skill Gaps", "PM arranges training or resource replacement", "SM provides coaching and training resources; team coaches each other"), listOf("Performance Measurement", "On time, on budget, as specified (EVM)", "Value delivered; customer satisfaction; working product"), listOf("Project Closure", "Formal closure even for early termination", "Continuous delivery; retrospective reviews; no single \"closure\""))),
                s("The \"Most Likely Wrong Answer\" Patterns", "PMI exam writers use consistent patterns for distractor (wrong) answers. Learn to spot them:"),
                s("The \"Most Likely Wrong Answer\" Patterns", tableHeaders = listOf("Pattern", "Why It Is Wrong", "Example"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Immediate drastic action", "Skips assessment; violates A.R.T.", "\"Immediately terminate the vendor contract\""), listOf("Escalate first", "PM abdicates responsibility", "\"Report the issue to the sponsor right away\""), listOf("Ignore / do nothing", "Never proactive", "\"Continue as planned and monitor\""), listOf("Bypass process", "Violates governance", "\"Make the change without formal approval since it is small\""), listOf("Blame or remove people", "Violates servant leadership", "\"Replace the underperforming team member\""), listOf("Absolute language", "PM is contextual, not absolute", "\"Always require formal documentation for all decisions\""), listOf("PM does the work", "PM manages; team executes", "\"The PM should personally fix the defect\""), listOf("Reactive over proactive", "PMI wants anticipation", "\"Wait for the issue to become critical before acting\""), listOf("Decide alone", "PMI wants collaboration", "\"The PM should decide the technical approach\""))),
                s("Ethics — PMI Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct", "Ethics questions appear in all three domains. The PMI Code has four values. Always choose the option that upholds ALL four:"),
                s("Ethics — PMI Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct", tableHeaders = listOf("Value", "Definition", "Exam Application"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Responsibility", "Take ownership of decisions and consequences; report unethical conduct", "If you discover a problem, you own reporting it — even if inconvenient"), listOf("Respect", "Treat all people with dignity; listen to others; negotiate in good faith", "No discrimination; no bullying; respect cultural differences"), listOf("Fairness", "Make decisions impartially; disclose conflicts of interest; no favouritism", "Declare conflicts of interest; treat vendors equally in procurement"), listOf("Honesty", "Be truthful in communications and conduct; do not deceive", "Never misreport project status; never hide bad news from stakeholders"))),
                s("Ethics — PMI Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct", "Exam rule: When an ethics question offers a \"convenient\" option and an \"honest but difficult\" option — always choose the honest one. PMI never rewards convenience over integrity.", SectionKind.DANGER),
                s("Common Ethics Scenarios", tableHeaders = listOf("Scenario", "Correct Response"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("You discover a colleague is falsifying test results", "Report it through appropriate channels — do not confront alone or ignore"), listOf("A vendor offers you a gift during procurement", "Decline; report per organisational policy; ensure no conflict of interest"), listOf("Sponsor asks you to report project status as \"green\" when it is actually \"red\"", "Refuse; report accurately; explain consequences of misreporting"), listOf("You are asked to work in a country with different ethics norms", "Follow PMI Code of Ethics regardless of local practices; escalate if conflict"), listOf("You have a personal relationship with a bidding vendor", "Disclose the conflict of interest immediately; recuse from selection decision"), listOf("A team member from a different culture is being excluded", "Address the exclusion; foster inclusion; apply cultural intelligence"))),
                s("Emotional Intelligence (EI) — Tested in People Domain", tableHeaders = listOf("EI Component", "Definition", "PM Application"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Self-Awareness", "Understand your own emotions, strengths, weaknesses, and their impact", "Recognise when stress affects your decision-making"), listOf("Self-Regulation", "Control impulses and moods; think before acting", "Do not react angrily in a heated meeting; pause and assess"), listOf("Motivation", "Drive to achieve beyond money or status; commitment and optimism", "Maintain team morale during setbacks"), listOf("Empathy", "Understand others' emotions; treat people according to their needs", "Recognise when a team member is struggling personally"), listOf("Social Skills", "Manage relationships; build networks; find common ground", "Resolve conflicts; influence without authority"))),
                s("Cultural Intelligence — Virtual and Global Teams", "• Recognise differing communication styles (direct vs indirect, hierarchical vs flat)\n• Adapt communication frequency and format to cultural preferences\n• Avoid scheduling important meetings during religious or national holidays of team members\n• Use visual communication (diagrams, dashboards) to overcome language barriers\n• Never assume one culture's norms are \"correct\" — this is bias\n• Build a team charter that explicitly documents agreed norms respecting all cultures"),
                s("Worked example — A.R.T. Framework — 5 Scenario Walk-throughs", "Scenario 1: \"The project is 3 weeks behind schedule.\"\n\nAssess: Which activities are delayed? Is it the critical path? What caused the delay? (Resource shortage? Underestimation? Dependency?)\n\nReview: What does the Schedule Management Plan say about recovery? Is there contingency? What does the Risk Register say (was this risk identified)?\n\nTake Action: Determine options (fast-track, crash, re-scope). Present to sponsor with impact. Raise change request if baseline needs to change.\n\n→ WRONG first action: \"Immediately crash the critical path.\" (Skips assessment of WHY and what options exist)\n\nScenario 2: \"A team member finds a serious defect in a module that was already accepted by the customer.\"\n\nAssess: How serious is it? (Data loss? Security? Minor display issue?) Does it affect other modules? When was it introduced?\n\nReview: Quality Management Plan — what is the defect severity classification? Was this defect covered by test cases?\n\nTake Action: Classify severity, raise a defect repair change request, communicate to customer based on severity, implement fix, regression test.\n\n→ WRONG first action: \"Immediately tell the customer.\" (Before you understand the severity and have a fix ready, this creates panic)\n\nScenario 3: \"The sponsor calls and says 'I heard the project is behind. Is it true?'\"\n\nAssess: Is it true? By how much? Do you have current data?\n\nReview: The latest Work Performance Report and EVM data. What does the data actually say?\n\nTake Action: Be honest. If behind: \"Yes, we are 2 weeks behind due to X. Here is our recovery plan: [specific actions]. We will be back on track by [date].\" If NOT behind: \"Our current data shows SPI=1.02 — we are slightly ahead. Here is the source of that information.\"\n\n→ WRONG action: \"Tell the sponsor everything is fine\" (dishonest if it is not, or confusing if you do not know yet)\n\nScenario 4: \"A vendor says they cannot deliver by the contracted date.\"\n\nAssess: How late? Why? Is this force majeure or vendor failure? What is the contract term? How does this affect the critical path?\n\nReview: Procurement Management Plan and the contract itself. What are the remedies, cure periods, and escalation paths?\n\nTake Action: Meet with the vendor face-to-face. Explore recovery options (expedite, partial delivery, substitute). If contractual breach — invoke remedies. Update risk register with new situation.\n\nScenario 5: \"Two stakeholders are lobbying for contradictory features.\"\n\nAssess: What are the actual requirements beh...", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Worked example — PMI \"Always/Never\" Pattern Recognition — 20 Quick Rules", "ALWAYS (almost always the correct answer):\n\n1. Assess BEFORE acting\n\n2. Communicate PROACTIVELY — before problems escalate\n\n3. Follow the PROCESS — even under pressure\n\n4. COLLABORATE before escalating\n\n5. Update the RISK REGISTER for new potential future events\n\n6. Update the ISSUE LOG for problems that have already occurred\n\n7. Get FORMAL WRITTEN acceptance before closing or transitioning\n\n8. Submit a CHANGE REQUEST — even for small changes\n\n9. Meet INDIVIDUALLY with conflicting parties before bringing them together\n\n10. Let the TEAM ESTIMATE (SMEs have the expertise, not the PM)\n\nNEVER (almost always the wrong answer):\n\n1. Never implement a change without CCB approval\n\n2. Never report status dishonestly — even under sponsor pressure\n\n3. Never fire/remove a team member as a FIRST response\n\n4. Never bypass the team's authority over their own work\n\n5. Never accept a gift from a vendor during procurement\n\n6. Never ignore a safety issue — STOP all work first\n\n7. Never skip lessons learned — even on a failed project\n\n8. Never release the team before formal acceptance is obtained\n\n9. Never use management reserve without management approval\n\n10. Never present AI-generated outputs without human validation\n\nThe pattern to recognize:\n\nIf an answer contains \"immediately,\" \"right away,\" \"without,\" or \"bypass\" — it is almost certainly wrong.\n\nIf an answer contains \"assess,\" \"review,\" \"communicate,\" \"collaborate,\" or \"formal\" — it is likely correct.", SectionKind.TIP),
            )
        ),
        Chapter(
            "integration",
            "🔗 Integration Management (Governance Domain)",
            "Integration in PMBOK 8",
            "Knowledge domains",
            listOf(
                s("Integration in PMBOK 8", "Integration Management — now part of the PMBOK 8 Governance performance domain — is the glue that holds all other domains together. The PM's primary role IS integration: ensuring all components work coherently toward project objectives."),
                s("Project Charter — Everything to Know", tableHeaders = listOf("Aspect", "Detail"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Purpose", "Formally authorises the project and grants the PM authority to apply organisational resources"), listOf("Created by", "The Sponsor — not the PM (PM may assist in drafting it)"), listOf("Created when", "During the Initiating Focus Area — before planning begins"), listOf("Key contents", "Project purpose and justification, measurable objectives, high-level requirements, high-level milestones, budget summary, key stakeholders, PM authority level, assumptions and constraints"), listOf("Guidance level", "High-level — refer to it when \"high-level guidance\" is needed in exam questions"))),
                s("Project Charter — Everything to Know", "Project Charter = PM's authority document. Without it, the PM has no authority. The Project Management Plan = detailed guidance. Know when to reference which.", SectionKind.KEY),
                s("Project Management Plan", "• The master integrating document — consolidates all subsidiary management plans and baselines\n• Baselines: Scope Baseline (scope statement + WBS + WBS dictionary), Schedule Baseline, Cost Baseline\n• Once formally approved, ALL changes require Integrated Change Control — even \"minor\" ones\n• It is PROGRESSIVE — updated throughout the project, not just written once and forgotten\n• Subsidiary plans: scope, schedule, cost, quality, resource, communications, risk, procurement, stakeholder management plans"),
                s("Project Management Plan", "When \"overall guidance\" or \"detailed guidance\" is needed — refer to the Project Management Plan. When only \"high-level guidance\" is needed — refer to the Project Charter.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Integrated Change Control — The Most Tested Process", "Change Identified\n\nRaise Change Request\n\nAssess Impact (scope/schedule/cost/quality)\n\nCCB Reviews\n\nApprove or Reject\n\nUpdate Plans & Baselines\n\nCommunicate Outcome"),
                s("Integrated Change Control — The Most Tested Process", "ALL changes go through ICC — no exceptions. Sponsor request, executive directive, \"minor\" addition — all require a formal change request and CCB approval. This is non-negotiable in PMI's world.", SectionKind.DANGER),
                s("Key Project Documents — Know the Differences", tableHeaders = listOf("Document", "Purpose", "When Updated"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Issue Log", "Tracks problems that have ALREADY occurred or are occurring", "First thing when any issue arises — before taking action"), listOf("Risk Register", "Documents identified risks, analysis, and planned responses", "New risk identified; risk status changes; during monitoring"), listOf("Assumption Log", "Documents project assumptions and constraints throughout", "Throughout planning and execution as assumptions are identified"), listOf("Lessons Learned Register", "Captures knowledge continuously — NOT just at project close", "When issues resolved; at phase gates; and at project closure"), listOf("Change Log", "Tracks all change requests and their decisions/status", "Each time a change request is submitted or decided"), listOf("Work Performance Data (WPD)", "Raw observations from executing: % complete, costs incurred, start/finish dates", "Continuously during executing — raw input"), listOf("Work Performance Information (WPI)", "Analyzed WPD — EVM metrics, SV, CV, SPI, CPI, variances", "During M&C after analyzing WPD"), listOf("Work Performance Reports (WPR)", "Formatted communication of WPI to stakeholders", "As per communications management plan frequency"))),
                s("Key Project Documents — Know the Differences", "Data → Information → Reports: WPD (raw) → analyzed into WPI → formatted into WPR for stakeholders. This sequence is tested.", SectionKind.NOTE),
                s("Project Closure — Sequence Matters", "Complete all work\n\nVerify/validate deliverables\n\nGet FORMAL stakeholder acceptance\n\nDocument lessons learned\n\nTransition to operations\n\nRelease resources\n\nClose contracts\n\nArchive documents"),
                s("Project Closure — Sequence Matters", "Formal acceptance of deliverables MUST come BEFORE lessons learned, BEFORE transition to operations, and BEFORE disbanding the team. This sequence is frequently tested.", SectionKind.DANGER),
                s("Change Request Types — Know All Four", tableHeaders = listOf("Type", "Definition", "Example", "Requires CCB?"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Corrective Action", "Realign project performance with the plan (fixing a problem that exists NOW)", "Adding resources to catch up on schedule", "Yes"), listOf("Preventive Action", "Reduce probability of future negative consequences (preventing a problem)", "Adding a quality checkpoint before a known risky phase", "Yes"), listOf("Defect Repair", "Fix a nonconforming product component", "Rewriting a module that failed testing", "Yes"), listOf("Update", "Modify formally controlled documents or plans", "Updating the schedule baseline after approved scope change", "Yes"))),
                s("Change Request Types — Know All Four", "ALL four types go through Integrated Change Control. Even \"preventive action\" — which sounds harmless — requires formal approval because it changes the plan.", SectionKind.WARN),
                s("Change Control Board (CCB) — Who and What", tableHeaders = listOf("Aspect", "Detail"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Who is on the CCB?", "Defined in the change management plan — typically sponsor, PM, key stakeholders, technical leads. The PM may or may not have voting authority."), listOf("What does CCB do?", "Reviews change requests; evaluates impact analysis; approves, rejects, or defers; records decisions"), listOf("Can PM approve changes alone?", "Only if explicitly authorised in the change management plan for specific types/thresholds. Otherwise — NO."), listOf("Emergency changes?", "Change management plan should define an expedited process. Even emergencies follow a (faster) process — they do not bypass it."), listOf("Change vs. Baseline", "Approved changes update the baseline. Rejected changes are logged with rationale. All are tracked in the Change Log."))),
                s("Configuration Management vs. Change Management", "Configuration Management\n • Manages the specifications of deliverables and processes\n• Identifies which items are under configuration control\n• Tracks versions, status, and audit trail of configuration items\n• Example: Version 2.3 of the design document is the approved baseline"),
                s("Change Management", "• Manages changes to baselines and project documents\n• Governs the process of raising, evaluating, approving changes\n• Change Control Board authority defined here\n• Example: A request to move from Version 2.3 to 2.4 must go through CCB"),
                s("Project Management Information System (PMIS)", "• Automated system used to collect, store, distribute project information\n• Includes: scheduling software, work authorisation systems, configuration management systems, information collection/distribution systems\n• PMBOK 8: PMIS increasingly leverages AI for real-time tracking, analysis, and reporting\n• Common exam point: PMIS is a TOOL/TECHNIQUE, not an output. It is an EEF (part of the organisational infrastructure)."),
                s("Phase Gates / Stage Gates", tableHeaders = listOf("Aspect", "Detail"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Definition", "Review at end of a phase where a decision is made to continue, modify, or terminate the project"), listOf("Also called", "Kill points, milestones, phase reviews, decision gates, toll gates"), listOf("Who decides?", "Typically the sponsor or steering committee — not the PM alone"), listOf("What is reviewed?", "Deliverables, performance vs plan, benefits realisation progress, business case validity, risks"), listOf("PMBOK 8 view", "Phase gates are tailoring decisions — used more in predictive; adaptive uses sprint reviews as lightweight gates"))),
                s("Worked example — Real-World Example: Integrated Change Control in Practice", "Scenario: You are PM for a ₹4 crore core banking system upgrade at a regional bank. Mid-execution, the CTO walks into your war room and says: \"Our auditors need a new transaction audit trail module — add it to the project. We need it in 3 months.\"\n\nWhat the PM does (step by step):\n\n1. Listen and acknowledge — \"Understood. Let me assess the impact and come back to you by end of week.\"\n\n2. Raise a formal Change Request — document: description, justification, requestor\n\n3. Impact Analysis:\n\n   • Scope: ~800 hours of new development + testing\n\n   • Schedule: +6 weeks to the critical path (not 3 months as requested)\n\n   • Cost: +₹38 lakh (dev, testing, infra)\n\n   • Quality risk: Rushing audit trail = regulatory liability\n\n   • Risk: External audit dependency; data privacy compliance needed\n\n4. CCB Review: Present options — (A) Full audit trail in 10 weeks, +₹38L; (B) Basic audit trail in 6 weeks, +₹18L, deferred enhancements later\n\n5. CCB Decision: Option B approved\n\n6. Update baselines: Scope baseline (new WBS element), Schedule baseline (+6 weeks), Cost baseline (+₹18L)\n\n7. Communicate: Inform all stakeholders of the approved change and new dates\n\nWhat the PM does NOT do: Tell the team \"CTO said add it\" and start work without formal approval.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Worked example — Project Documents — Decision Guide", "Question: \"A team member finds a new risk. What document do you update first?\"\n\nAnswer: Risk Register → then analyze → then plan response → then update the Risk Management Plan if the approach changes.\n\nQuestion: \"A vendor delivers a component late. What document do you update?\"\n\nAnswer: Issue Log (it has ALREADY happened) → then assess schedule impact → then corrective action/change request.\n\nQuestion: \"A sponsor's assumption about market conditions proves false. What do you update?\"\n\nAnswer: Assumption Log (assumption proved incorrect) → then Risk Register (if this creates new risks) → then review Business Case validity.\n\nMental model: Future uncertainty → Risk Register. Past/present problem → Issue Log. Assumption invalidated → Assumption Log. Process knowledge gained → Lessons Learned Register.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Worked example — WPD → WPI → WPR Flow — Fintech Example", "Example project: Mobile banking app, Sprint 14\n\nWork Performance DATA (WPD) — Raw facts collected during executing:\n\n• 8 of 12 planned stories completed\n\n• 142 hours logged, planned was 120 hours\n\n• 3 defects found in testing, 2 fixed, 1 open\n\n• Payment gateway integration still in progress\n\nWork Performance INFORMATION (WPI) — Analyzed during M&C:\n\n• SPI = EV/PV = (8/12) = 0.67 → 33% behind schedule\n\n• CPI = EV/AC = (8×10 points)/(142h) = needs baseline to compute in \$\n\n• Sprint Goal at risk: payment gateway at 0% with 3 days left\n\n• Root cause: dependency on vendor API documentation (delayed 5 days)\n\nWork Performance REPORT (WPR) — Formatted for stakeholders:\n\n• Executive Dashboard: \"Sprint 14 — Amber. Payment gateway delayed due to vendor dependency. Recovery plan: expedited API docs by Thursday, Saturday catch-up session. Expected completion: Monday.\"\n\n• Technical Report: Full defect log, story status, SPI/CPI, risk update\n\nKey point: The SAME raw data becomes different WPR formats for different audiences.", SectionKind.TIP),
            )
        ),
        Chapter(
            "scope",
            "📐 Scope Management",
            "Scope Terminology",
            "Knowledge domains",
            listOf(
                s("Scope Terminology", tableHeaders = listOf("Term", "Definition", "Exam Trap"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Product Scope", "Features and functions that characterise the product/service/result", "Different from Project Scope"), listOf("Project Scope", "Work required to deliver the product with specified features", "Includes product scope + all PM work"), listOf("Scope Creep", "Uncontrolled addition of features without change control — caused by poor requirements and weak change control", "Very common exam topic — always a problem"), listOf("Gold Plating", "Adding extras BEYOND agreed scope — even if well-intentioned", "Avoid! Wastes resources, introduces unreviewed risk, not what customer asked for"), listOf("Progressive Elaboration", "Continuously improving project plan detail as more info becomes available", "Same concept as Rolling Wave Planning"))),
                s("Requirements Classification (PMBOK 8 — Formalised)", tableHeaders = listOf("Type", "Description", "Example"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Business", "High-level strategic needs; WHY the project exists", "Increase market share by 15%"), listOf("Stakeholder", "Needs of a specific stakeholder or group", "CFO needs monthly cost variance reports"), listOf("Functional", "What the product MUST DO — behaviours, actions, data", "System must allow password reset via email link"), listOf("Nonfunctional", "HOW the product performs — performance, security, reliability, usability", "System must support 10,000 concurrent users with <2 sec response time"), listOf("Transition", "Temporary capabilities to move from current to desired future state", "Data migration scripts; user training program; parallel run period"))),
                s("Requirements Classification (PMBOK 8 — Formalised)", "Requirements must be: Unambiguous, Measurable, Testable, Traceable, Complete, Consistent, and Acceptable to key stakeholders — BEFORE baselining.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("WBS — Work Breakdown Structure", "• Definition: Hierarchical decomposition of total project scope into manageable work packages\n• 100% Rule: WBS must capture 100% of project scope — nothing more, nothing less\n• Work Package: Lowest-level WBS component — what gets estimated, scheduled, and assigned to a team\n• WBS Dictionary: Detailed description of each work package — owner, resources, duration, cost estimate, acceptance criteria\n• Planning Package: WBS component with known work content but not yet decomposed (used in rolling wave planning)\n• Scope Baseline = Project Scope Statement + WBS + WBS Dictionary (all three together)"),
                s("Validate Scope vs. Control Scope — Frequently Confused", "Validate Scope\n • Process of formalising ACCEPTANCE of completed deliverables\n• Done WITH the customer / sponsor / product owner\n• Input: Verified Deliverables (from Control Quality)\n• Output: Accepted Deliverables\n• Concerned with customer ACCEPTANCE\n• Performed in M&C Focus Area"),
                s("Control Scope", "• Process of monitoring scope status and managing changes to scope baseline\n• Done by the project team continuously\n• Input: Work Performance Data\n• Output: Work Performance Information, Change Requests\n• Concerned with PREVENTING scope creep\n• Performed continuously throughout the project"),
                s("Control Scope", "Critical Sequence: Control Quality → Verified Deliverables → Validate Scope → Accepted Deliverables. Control Quality is ALWAYS performed BEFORE Validate Scope. This sequence is frequently tested.", SectionKind.DANGER),
                s("Requirements Traceability Matrix (RTM)", "• Links requirements from origin through to final deliverable — ensures nothing is missed\n• Traces: business need → business requirement → project requirement → design → test → delivery\n• Key exam point: If a requirement is not in the RTM, it is not in scope\n• Managed as part of Scope Management throughout the project lifecycle"),
                s("Requirements Elicitation Techniques", tableHeaders = listOf("Technique", "Description", "Best For"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Interviews", "One-on-one or small group discussions to elicit requirements", "Detailed requirements from key stakeholders; sensitive topics"), listOf("Focus Groups", "Prequalified stakeholders and SMEs discuss expectations", "Understanding needs of a specific user group"), listOf("Facilitated Workshops", "Cross-functional stakeholders define requirements together (JAD sessions)", "Reconciling stakeholder differences; building consensus"), listOf("Brainstorming", "Generate ideas quickly without judgment", "Early-stage requirement discovery"), listOf("Nominal Group Technique", "Brainstorming + ranking/voting to prioritise", "Prioritising many ideas"), listOf("Delphi Technique", "Anonymous expert opinions gathered iteratively until consensus", "Avoiding groupthink; sensitive or political topics"), listOf("Observation (Job Shadowing)", "Watching users perform their work", "Discovering unstated requirements users cannot articulate"), listOf("Prototypes", "Working model of the expected product for early feedback", "Validating requirements when they are hard to describe"), listOf("Questionnaires / Surveys", "Written questions to large audience", "Many stakeholders; geographically dispersed; quantitative data"), listOf("Document Analysis", "Review existing documentation", "Regulatory requirements; existing system requirements"), listOf("Context Diagrams", "Visual model of system scope and interfaces", "Understanding system boundaries"), listOf("Benchmarking", "Compare practices to comparable organisations", "Identifying best practices"))),
                s("Prioritisation Techniques", tableHeaders = listOf("Technique", "How It Works", "Where Used"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("MoSCoW", "Must have / Should have / Could have / Won't have (this time)", "Agile backlogs; requirement prioritisation"), listOf("Kano Model", "Basic (expected) / Performance (more is better) / Excitement (delighters)", "Product feature prioritisation"), listOf("WSJF (Weighted Shortest Job First)", "Cost of Delay ÷ Job Size — highest score first", "SAFe; objective prioritisation"), listOf("Paired Comparison", "Compare each item against every other item", "Small sets of requirements"), listOf("100-Point Method", "Stakeholders distribute 100 points across requirements", "Stakeholder-driven prioritisation"), listOf("Multi-Criteria Decision Analysis", "Weighted scoring across multiple criteria", "Complex decisions with multiple factors"))),
                s("Scope in Agile vs. Predictive", tableHeaders = listOf("Aspect", "Predictive", "Agile"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Scope definition", "Fully defined upfront in scope statement + WBS", "Emerges through backlog refinement; high-level vision upfront"), listOf("Scope baseline", "Fixed; changes via formal change control", "Product backlog is dynamic; PO reprioritises continuously"), listOf("Scope validation", "Formal acceptance at milestones", "Sprint review acceptance of increment"), listOf("Scope creep control", "Change control process; CCB", "Sprint Goal protection; only PO changes backlog"), listOf("Decomposition", "WBS → work packages", "Theme → Epic → Story → Task"), listOf("Requirements doc", "Requirements documentation + RTM", "User stories with acceptance criteria"))),
                s("Definition of Ready vs. Definition of Done", "Definition of Ready (DoR)\n • Criteria a backlog item must meet BEFORE it can be pulled into a sprint\n• Example: story is clear, estimated, acceptance criteria defined, dependencies resolved\n• Prevents teams from starting work on poorly understood items\n• Not an official Scrum artifact but widely used"),
                s("Definition of Done (DoD)", "• Criteria an item must meet to be considered COMPLETE\n• Example: code reviewed, tested, documented, deployed to staging\n• Ensures consistent quality across all increments\n• Official Scrum artifact commitment for the Increment"),
                s("Worked example — Real-World Example: Scope Creep vs Gold Plating vs Legitimate Change", "Project: Customer-facing payments portal for a fintech startup\n\nScenario A — Scope Creep:\n\nThe customer casually mentions in a meeting: \"Oh, and can you add dark mode?\" The developer adds it over the weekend without a change request.\n\n→ This is SCOPE CREEP. Work was added without going through change control. Even if it takes only 4 hours — it sets a dangerous precedent, and now \"dark mode\" is in the product but not in any test plan, documentation, or baseline.\n\nScenario B — Gold Plating:\n\nThe developer decides to add animated micro-interactions to every button because \"it will delight users.\" The customer never asked for this.\n\n→ This is GOLD PLATING. The team is adding beyond agreed scope. It wastes time, adds untested features, and may not align with the customer's brand or performance requirements.\n\nScenario C — Legitimate Change:\n\nThe customer submits a written request for dark mode after seeing a competitor launch it. The PM raises a change request, assesses it (+3 days, +₹45,000), the CCB approves, and baselines are updated.\n\n→ This is the RIGHT way. Change is welcome — uncontrolled change is the problem.\n\nThe rule: Any addition to scope, however small, requires a change request. \"Small\" changes are where scope creep enters.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Worked example — WBS Creation — Step by Step Example", "Project: Launch a PMP® Exam Prep Online Course\n\nLevel 1 — Project: PMP Exam Prep Course Launch\n\nLevel 2 — Major Deliverables:\n\n1.0 Course Content\n\n2.0 Learning Management System (LMS)\n\n3.0 Marketing\n\n4.0 Project Management\n\nLevel 3 — Components:\n\n1.1 Study Notes (21 modules)\n\n1.2 Video Lectures (21 modules × 30 min)\n\n1.3 Practice Questions (500+)\n\n1.4 Mock Exams (3 × 180 questions)\n\n2.1 Platform selection and setup\n\n2.2 Content upload and QA\n\n2.3 Payment integration\n\n3.1 Landing page design\n\n3.2 SEO and content marketing\n\n3.3 LinkedIn/YouTube launch campaign\n\nLevel 4 — Work Packages (lowest level):\n\n1.1.1 Write PMBOK 8 module (owner: Sushant; est: 8h; cost: ₹8,000)\n\n1.1.2 Review and edit PMBOK 8 module (owner: Reviewer; est: 2h; cost: ₹2,000)\n\n100% Rule check: Does the WBS include everything in the scope statement? Does it include ONLY what is in the scope statement? If yes to both — the WBS passes.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Worked example — Requirements Elicitation — Choosing the Right Technique", "Situation 1: You are gathering requirements for a new core banking system that will be used by 2,000 branch staff across India. Most cannot be interviewed individually.\n\n→ Best technique: Survey/Questionnaire — reach large, dispersed audience efficiently. Follow up with Focus Groups in 3–4 regional hubs for depth.\n\nSituation 2: The CFO and CTO disagree on what \"real-time reconciliation\" means for the system.\n\n→ Best technique: Facilitated Workshop (JAD session) — bring both together with a neutral facilitator to define the term with measurable acceptance criteria. Ground rules: no hierarchy in the room, decisions documented immediately.\n\nSituation 3: Branch tellers say the current system is \"fine\" but complaints data shows 40% of errors happen during account opening. They cannot articulate what goes wrong.\n\n→ Best technique: Observation (Job Shadowing) — watch tellers open accounts in real time. You will see the unstated workflow steps, workarounds, and friction points they cannot describe in a meeting.\n\nSituation 4: You need to know if customers would prefer mobile biometric authentication, OTP, or PIN for transaction confirmation. 15,000 users need to be sampled.\n\n→ Best technique: Survey/Questionnaire + Data Analysis — quantitative data from a large sample, then prototype-test the top choice with a Focus Group.\n\nRemember: The BEST technique depends on WHO you need to reach, HOW many, and WHAT type of requirement (explicit vs implicit vs unstated).", SectionKind.TIP),
            )
        ),
        Chapter(
            "schedule",
            "📅 Schedule Management",
            "Schedule Development Flow",
            "Knowledge domains",
            listOf(
                s("Schedule Development Flow", "Plan Schedule Mgmt\n\nDefine Activities\n\nSequence Activities\n\nEstimate Durations\n\nDevelop Schedule\n\nControl Schedule"),
                s("Critical Path Method (CPM)", "• Critical Path: The LONGEST path through the network diagram — determines the earliest possible project completion date\n• Total Float/Slack: Amount of time an activity can be delayed without delaying the project end date\n• Critical Path activities = ZERO total float — any delay to them delays the entire project\n• Free Float: Time an activity can delay without delaying its immediate SUCCESSOR (may be different from total float)\n• Negative Float: Project is already behind schedule — critical path activities are late\n• Near-critical path: Paths with very low float — monitor closely as they could become critical"),
                s("Critical Path Method (CPM)", "If a question asks which activities to prioritise — ALWAYS prioritise critical path activities (zero float) first. Non-critical activities have slack that can absorb minor delays.", SectionKind.KEY),
                s("Logical Relationship Types", tableHeaders = listOf("Type", "Abbrev.", "Meaning", "Most Common?"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Finish-to-Start", "FS", "Successor cannot START until predecessor FINISHES", "✅ Most common — default assumption"), listOf("Finish-to-Finish", "FF", "Successor cannot FINISH until predecessor FINISHES", "Common in testing scenarios"), listOf("Start-to-Start", "SS", "Successor cannot START until predecessor STARTS", "Common in parallel work"), listOf("Start-to-Finish", "SF", "Successor cannot FINISH until predecessor STARTS", "Rare — almost never used"))),
                s("Lead and Lag", "Lead (Negative Lag — Overlap)\n • Activity B starts BEFORE Activity A is complete\n• Creates an overlap between activities\n• Shown as negative lag in scheduling tools\n• REDUCES overall project duration\n• Example: Landscaping starts 2 weeks before punch list completion (FS –2 weeks)"),
                s("Lag (Waiting Time — Delay)", "• A mandatory wait BETWEEN activities\n• Successor must wait after predecessor starts or finishes\n• EXTENDS overall project duration\n• Duration estimates do NOT include lead or lag time\n• Example: Editing starts 15 days after writing begins (SS +15 days)"),
                s("Schedule Compression Techniques", tableHeaders = listOf("Technique", "What It Does", "Cost Impact", "Risk Impact", "Applied To", "When to Use"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Crashing", "Add resources (people, equipment, overtime) to shorten duration of critical path activities", "Increases cost — sometimes significantly", "Moderate — more people = more coordination", "Critical path activities ONLY — no value on non-critical", "When schedule MUST be shortened and budget allows"), listOf("Fast Tracking", "Perform sequential activities in parallel that were originally planned sequentially", "No direct additional cost", "Increases rework risk — if earlier work changes, later parallel work may need redoing", "Only where logical overlap is possible", "When schedule must be shortened without adding cost"))),
                s("Schedule Compression Techniques", "Always consider Fast Tracking BEFORE Crashing — it does not add direct cost. Use Crashing only when fast tracking is not possible or insufficient.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Resource Leveling vs. Resource Smoothing", tableHeaders = listOf("Resource Leveling", "Resource Smoothing"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Purpose", "Resolve resource over-allocation or conflicts", "Optimise resource usage within float available"), listOf("Effect on Schedule", "MAY extend the schedule", "Does NOT extend the schedule beyond current dates"), listOf("Float", "May consume total float and change critical path", "Only uses available float — critical path unchanged"), listOf("When Used", "When resources are constrained and overallocated", "When schedule dates are fixed but resource usage needs smoothing"))),
                s("Estimation Techniques", tableHeaders = listOf("Technique", "Method", "Accuracy Range", "When to Use"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Analogous", "Historical data from similar projects; expert judgment; top-down approach", "Low: –25% to +75%", "Early project; when little detail is available; quick estimate needed"), listOf("Parametric", "Algorithm using historical data × project parameters (e.g., cost per unit × quantity)", "Medium to High", "When measurable, repeatable parameters exist (construction, software features)"), listOf("Three-Point (PERT)", "Optimistic (O), Most Likely (M), Pessimistic (P) — accounts for uncertainty", "High", "High uncertainty; insufficient historical data; risk-aware estimation"), listOf("Bottom-Up", "Estimate each work package individually and aggregate upward", "Highest: –5% to +10%", "When detailed scope is defined; most accurate but most time-consuming"))),
                s("Estimation Techniques", "Estimation Formulas"),
                s("Rolling Wave Planning", "• Near-term work is planned in detail; far future work is planned at a higher level\n• Detail is added progressively as the project moves forward and more information becomes available\n• NOT a sign of poor planning — it is deliberately embraced in uncertain environments\n• Used in both predictive (for future phases) and adaptive (sprint-by-sprint) approaches\n• Also called Progressive Elaboration when referring to the planning process itself"),
                s("Forward and Backward Pass — Step by Step", "The Critical Path Method uses forward and backward passes to calculate early/late dates and float:"),
                s("Forward and Backward Pass — Step by Step", tableHeaders = listOf("Step", "Calculation", "What You Get"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Forward Pass", "ES = max(EF of all predecessors). EF = ES + Duration − 1 (or ES + Duration if using 0-based)", "Early Start (ES) and Early Finish (EF) for every activity"), listOf("Backward Pass", "LF = min(LS of all successors). LS = LF − Duration + 1", "Late Start (LS) and Late Finish (LF) for every activity"), listOf("Total Float", "TF = LS − ES (or LF − EF — same result)", "How much an activity can slip without delaying project"), listOf("Free Float", "FF = min(ES of successors) − EF", "How much an activity can slip without delaying its immediate successor"))),
                s("Worked example — Forward and Backward Pass — Step by Step", "Worked Example (0-based convention):\n\nActivities: A (dur 3) → B (dur 4) → D (dur 2). A (dur 3) → C (dur 6) → D (dur 2).\n\nForward Pass:\n\nA: ES=0, EF=3\n\nB: ES=3, EF=7  |  C: ES=3, EF=9\n\nD: ES = max(7, 9) = 9, EF = 11\n\nProject duration = 11\n\nBackward Pass (start from D: LF=11):\n\nD: LF=11, LS=9\n\nB: LF=9, LS=5  |  C: LF=9, LS=3\n\nA: LF = min(5, 3) = 3, LS=0\n\nFloat:\n\nA: LS−ES = 0−0 = 0 (critical)\n\nB: LS−ES = 5−3 = 2 (has 2 days float)\n\nC: LS−ES = 3−3 = 0 (critical)\n\nD: LS−ES = 9−9 = 0 (critical)\n\nCritical Path: A → C → D (11 days). B has 2 days float.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Dependency Types", tableHeaders = listOf("Type", "Definition", "Example"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Mandatory (Hard Logic)", "Inherent in the nature of work; physically or legally required", "Foundation must be poured before walls are built"), listOf("Discretionary (Soft/Preferred Logic)", "Based on best practice or team preference; can be changed", "Team prefers to complete design before starting any coding"), listOf("External", "Dependency on non-project activities outside team control", "Waiting for government permit approval"), listOf("Internal", "Dependency within the project team's control", "Testing cannot begin until development completes"))),
                s("Dependency Types", "When fast-tracking, look for DISCRETIONARY dependencies to overlap — mandatory dependencies cannot be fast-tracked.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Schedule Tools and Charts", tableHeaders = listOf("Tool", "Purpose", "Key Feature"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Gantt Chart (Bar Chart)", "Visual timeline of activities", "Shows start/end dates, durations, overlaps — easy for stakeholders"), listOf("Milestone Chart", "Shows only major milestones", "High-level view for executives; zero-duration events"), listOf("Network Diagram (PDM)", "Shows logical relationships between activities", "Used for critical path analysis; precedence diagramming method"), listOf("Schedule Baseline", "Approved version of the schedule", "Used to measure actual performance against plan"), listOf("Resource Histogram", "Shows resource usage over time", "Identifies over-allocation and under-utilisation"), listOf("Monte Carlo Simulation", "Probabilistic schedule modelling", "Shows probability of finishing by various dates"))),
                s("Agile Scheduling Concepts", tableHeaders = listOf("Concept", "Definition", "Formula/Usage"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Timebox", "Fixed duration for an activity or iteration; scope flexes, time does not", "Sprints (1–4 weeks), Daily Scrum (15 min)"), listOf("Velocity", "Story points completed per sprint", "Forecast: Remaining points ÷ velocity = sprints remaining"), listOf("Release Planning", "Determining which features go in which release", "Based on velocity and backlog priority"), listOf("Iteration Burndown", "Shows work remaining in a sprint", "Ideal line vs actual line — gap = ahead/behind"), listOf("Release Burnup", "Shows work completed toward release + total scope", "Scope line moves up when scope added"), listOf("On-demand scheduling", "Kanban — pull work when capacity available", "No timeboxes; continuous flow"))),
                s("Agile Scheduling Concepts", "Agile Forecasting"),
                s("Worked example — Forward and Backward Pass — Complete Worked Example", "Network: A→B, A→C, B→D, C→D, D→E\n\nDurations: A=3, B=4, C=6, D=2, E=3\n\nFORWARD PASS (Early dates, 0-based end-of-day convention):\n\nA: ES=0, EF=0+3=3\n\nB: ES=3 (after A), EF=3+4=7\n\nC: ES=3 (after A), EF=3+6=9\n\nD: ES=max(EF_B, EF_C)=max(7,9)=9, EF=9+2=11\n\nE: ES=11 (after D), EF=11+3=14\n\nProject Duration = 14 days\n\nBACKWARD PASS (Late dates, working back from LF=14):\n\nE: LF=14, LS=14-3=11\n\nD: LF=11 (before E), LS=11-2=9\n\nC: LF=9 (before D), LS=9-6=3\n\nB: LF=9 (before D), LS=9-4=5\n\nA: LF=min(LS_B, LS_C)=min(5,3)=3, LS=3-3=0\n\nFLOAT CALCULATION (TF = LS - ES):\n\nA: TF=0-0=0 → CRITICAL\n\nB: TF=5-3=2 → Has float, NOT critical\n\nC: TF=3-3=0 → CRITICAL\n\nD: TF=9-9=0 → CRITICAL\n\nE: TF=11-11=0 → CRITICAL\n\nCritical Path: A → C → D → E (14 days)\n\nActivity B has 2 days of float — it can be delayed up to 2 days without delaying the project.\n\nIf B is delayed by 3 days: B now finishes at day 10. D's ES = max(10,9) = 10. D finishes at 12. E starts 12, finishes 15. Project is now 15 days → 1 day delay.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Worked example — Crashing vs Fast Tracking — Decision Framework", "Scenario: Your 20-day project needs to be done in 16 days. Critical path: A(4)→B(6)→C(5)→D(5). Non-critical path: A(4)→E(3)→D(5) — total 12 days with 8 days float.\n\nOption 1: Fast Track\n\nCan C and D overlap? If C→D dependency is discretionary (C should finish before D starts, but not mandatory): Start D when C is 50% complete (3 days). New duration for C+D: 3+5=8 days instead of 10. Project: 4+6+8=18 days. Saved 2 days, no cost.\n\nCan B and C overlap? B→C is mandatory (design must be done). Cannot fast-track.\n\nResult: Fast tracking C/D gives 18 days — need 2 more days saved.\n\nOption 2: Crash B\n\nB is 6 days. Adding 2 resources reduces it to 4 days at extra cost of ₹50,000.\n\nNew duration: 4+4+8=16 days. ✅ Target met.\n\nTotal cost increase: ₹50,000.\n\nPM Decision Framework:\n\n1. Try fast tracking first (free) on discretionary dependencies only\n\n2. If not enough, crash the critical path activity with the LOWEST cost per day saved\n\n3. Only crash activities ON the critical path — crashing non-critical activities wastes money\n\n4. After each crash, recalculate critical path — another path may become critical", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Worked example — Agile Velocity Forecasting — Real Example", "Product Backlog: 240 story points remaining\n\nSprint length: 2 weeks\n\nVelocity history (last 5 sprints): 22, 18, 25, 20, 20\n\nStep 1 — Calculate average velocity:\n\n(22+18+25+20+20) / 5 = 105 / 5 = 21 story points/sprint\n\nStep 2 — Estimate sprints remaining:\n\n240 / 21 = 11.4 → round UP to 12 sprints (you cannot deliver partial sprints)\n\nStep 3 — Estimate calendar time:\n\n12 sprints × 2 weeks = 24 weeks = approximately 6 months\n\nStep 4 — Reality checks:\n\n• Does the team have any planned leave in those 6 months? (Reduce available capacity)\n\n• Is scope likely to grow? (Burnup chart would show scope additions)\n\n• Is velocity stable? High variance (18–25) suggests forecasts have ±15% uncertainty\n\nCommunication to sponsor:\n\n\"Based on current velocity, we forecast completing all 240 points in approximately 12 sprints (6 months). This assumes stable team composition and no significant scope additions. We recommend reviewing the forecast every 3 sprints.\"", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Worked example — Schedule Compression Decision Tree", "Project is behind schedule. Apply this sequence:\n\nStep 1: Which activities are on the critical path? (Only these matter for compression)\n\nStep 2: Can any critical path activities be fast-tracked (overlapped)?\n\n→ YES: Check if dependency is DISCRETIONARY. If yes, overlap by 25–50%. Monitor rework risk.\n\n→ NO: All dependencies are mandatory. Go to Step 3.\n\nStep 3: Can any critical path activities be crashed (resources added)?\n\n→ YES: Calculate crash cost per day for each critical activity. Crash the CHEAPEST first.\n\nExample: Activity B costs ₹10,000/day to crash. Activity C costs ₹25,000/day. Crash B first.\n\n→ NO: Cannot add resources. Go to Step 4.\n\nStep 4: Can scope be reduced (remove low-priority requirements)?\n\n→ Requires formal change request. Sponsor/customer must agree.\n\nStep 5: Escalate to sponsor with options and cost. Sponsor decides.\n\nNEVER: Cut quality testing, hide the delay, or work the team unsustainable hours without addressing the root cause.", SectionKind.TIP),
            )
        ),
        Chapter(
            "cost",
            "💰 Cost Management & Earned Value (EVM)",
            "Key Cost Concepts",
            "Knowledge domains",
            listOf(
                s("Danger", "EVM questions appear in almost every PMP exam. You MUST know ALL formulas cold AND understand what each result means. Practice with numbers until it becomes automatic.", SectionKind.DANGER),
                s("Key Cost Concepts", tableHeaders = listOf("Term", "Definition", "Memory Aid"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("BAC — Budget at Completion", "Total approved budget for the entire project", "The \"target\" — what you planned to spend in total"), listOf("PV — Planned Value", "Authorised budget for scheduled work at a specific point in time (BCWS)", "What you PLANNED to accomplish by now (in \$)"), listOf("EV — Earned Value", "Planned value of work actually completed: EV = % Complete × BAC", "What you have EARNED by completing work (in \$)"), listOf("AC — Actual Cost", "Actual cost incurred for work performed (ACWP)", "What you ACTUALLY SPENT so far"), listOf("EAC — Estimate at Completion", "Projected total cost at project completion (forecast)", "How much the project will COST IN TOTAL"), listOf("ETC — Estimate to Complete", "Expected cost to finish all REMAINING work", "How much MORE you need to spend"), listOf("VAC — Variance at Completion", "BAC − EAC — projected over/under budget at end", "Positive = under budget. Negative = over budget at end."), listOf("TCPI — To-Complete Performance Index", "Cost efficiency needed to meet financial target", "How HARD you must work from here to meet BAC or EAC"))),
                s("Key Cost Concepts", "EVM Formulas — All of Them"),
                s("Quick Interpretation Table", tableHeaders = listOf("Metric", "Positive ✅", "Zero = On Track", "Negative ❌"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("SV (EV−PV)", "Ahead of schedule", "Exactly on schedule", "Behind schedule"), listOf("CV (EV−AC)", "Under budget", "Exactly on budget", "Over budget"), listOf("SPI (EV/PV)", ">1.0 ahead of schedule", "=1.0 on schedule", "<1.0 behind schedule"), listOf("CPI (EV/AC)", ">1.0 under budget", "=1.0 on budget", "<1.0 over budget"), listOf("TCPI", "<1.0 easier than current rate", "=1.0 maintain current efficiency", ">1.0 must work MORE efficiently than now"), listOf("VAC (BAC−EAC)", "Positive = will finish under budget", "Negative = will finish over budget"))),
                s("Worked example — Fully Worked EVM Example", "Scenario: Project BAC = \$200,000. After 6 months, 50% should be complete (PV = \$100,000). Actual work completed = 40% (EV = \$80,000). Actual cost = \$110,000 (AC).\n\nEV = 40% × \$200,000 = \$80,000\n\nSV = EV − PV = 80,000 − 100,000 = −\$20,000 → Behind schedule\n\nCV = EV − AC = 80,000 − 110,000 = −\$30,000 → Over budget\n\nSPI = EV / PV = 80,000 / 100,000 = 0.80 → Only 80% schedule efficiency\n\nCPI = EV / AC = 80,000 / 110,000 = 0.727 → Only 72.7 cents of value per \$1 spent\n\nEAC (typical) = BAC / CPI = 200,000 / 0.727 = \$275,104\n\nETC = EAC − AC = 275,104 − 110,000 = \$165,104 → Still need to spend this much\n\nVAC = BAC − EAC = 200,000 − 275,104 = −\$75,104 → Will be ~\$75K over budget at end\n\nTCPI (to meet BAC) = (200,000 − 80,000) / (200,000 − 110,000) = 120,000 / 90,000 = 1.33 → Must be 33% more efficient than current rate — very difficult", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Reserve Analysis", "Contingency Reserve\n • For known unknowns (identified risks in the risk register)\n• Managed by the PM — within PM's authority\n• Part of the project cost baseline\n• Triggered when a risk response plan is activated\n• Shown in cost baseline and schedule baseline"),
                s("Management Reserve", "• For unknown unknowns (unforeseeable risks)\n• Controlled by management/sponsor — NOT the PM\n• NOT part of PM's baseline — separate from baseline\n• Requires management approval to access\n• Not included in Earned Value calculations"),
                s("Management Reserve", "If a PM uses management reserve without approval — that is a problem. If a PM uses contingency reserve for an identified risk — that is correct. Know the difference.", SectionKind.WARN),
                s("Cost Estimation Techniques", tableHeaders = listOf("Technique", "Accuracy", "Notes"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("ROM (Rough Order of Magnitude)", "−25% to +75%", "Very early estimate; minimal information; used for feasibility"), listOf("Budget Estimate", "−10% to +25%", "Based on partial design or scope; better information"), listOf("Definitive Estimate", "−5% to +10%", "Detailed scope and design available; highest accuracy"), listOf("Parametric Estimating", "Varies", "Algorithm-based; highly accurate when historical data is good"))),
                s("EAC — Which Formula When?", tableHeaders = listOf("Scenario in Question", "Use This EAC Formula", "Why"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("\"Current variances are typical / expected to continue\"", "EAC = BAC / CPI", "Assumes current cost efficiency continues for the rest"), listOf("\"Original estimate was flawed\" or \"assumptions were wrong\"", "EAC = AC + Bottom-up ETC", "Re-estimate remaining work from scratch"), listOf("\"Current variances are atypical / one-time / will not recur\"", "EAC = AC + (BAC − EV)", "Remaining work will be done at PLANNED rate"), listOf("\"Both schedule and cost performance affect the estimate\"", "EAC = AC + (BAC − EV) / (CPI × SPI)", "Accounts for both indices"))),
                s("EAC — Which Formula When?", "The exam ALWAYS gives you a clue about which formula to use — read the scenario for words like \"typical,\" \"atypical,\" \"flawed,\" or \"both cost and schedule.\" If no clue is given, default to BAC / CPI.", SectionKind.KEY),
                s("Worked example — Second Worked Example — Recovering Project", "Scenario: BAC = \$500,000. Project is 60% complete. PV = \$350,000. AC = \$280,000.\n\nEV = 60% × 500,000 = \$300,000\n\nSV = 300,000 − 350,000 = −\$50,000 (behind schedule)\n\nCV = 300,000 − 280,000 = +\$20,000 (under budget!)\n\nSPI = 300,000 / 350,000 = 0.857 (behind schedule)\n\nCPI = 300,000 / 280,000 = 1.071 (under budget — spending efficiently)\n\nEAC (typical) = 500,000 / 1.071 = \$466,853\n\nVAC = 500,000 − 466,853 = +\$33,147 (will finish under budget)\n\nTCPI (to BAC) = (500,000 − 300,000) / (500,000 − 280,000) = 200,000 / 220,000 = 0.909\n\nInterpretation: This project is behind schedule but under budget. CPI > 1 means cost performance is good. TCPI < 1 means the team can afford to spend slightly more per unit of work than currently and still meet BAC. The schedule is the concern — consider fast-tracking (no cost) or crashing (has budget headroom).", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Cost Baseline vs. Budget", "Activity Cost Estimates\n\n+ Work Package Contingency\n\n= Work Package Cost\n\n+ Control Account Contingency\n\n= Cost Baseline\n\n+ Management Reserve\n\n= Project Budget"),
                s("Cost Baseline vs. Budget", tableHeaders = listOf("Component", "Included In", "Controlled By"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Activity cost estimates", "Cost baseline", "PM"), listOf("Contingency reserve", "Cost baseline", "PM"), listOf("Management reserve", "Project budget (NOT cost baseline)", "Sponsor/management"))),
                s("Cost Baseline vs. Budget", "Exam trap: \"Which is NOT part of the cost baseline?\" → Management Reserve. The cost baseline is what EVM measures against. Management reserve sits above the baseline.", SectionKind.WARN),
                s("Financial Analysis for Project Selection", tableHeaders = listOf("Metric", "Formula / Definition", "Decision Rule"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("NPV (Net Present Value)", "Sum of discounted future cash flows minus initial investment", "Higher NPV is better; NPV > 0 = worthwhile"), listOf("IRR (Internal Rate of Return)", "Discount rate at which NPV = 0", "Higher IRR is better; compare to hurdle rate"), listOf("Payback Period", "Time to recover the initial investment", "Shorter is better; ignores time value of money"), listOf("BCR (Benefit-Cost Ratio)", "PV of Benefits ÷ PV of Costs", "BCR > 1 = benefits exceed costs; higher is better"), listOf("ROI (Return on Investment)", "(Gain − Cost) ÷ Cost × 100%", "Higher is better"), listOf("Opportunity Cost", "Value of the next best alternative NOT chosen", "Lower opportunity cost is better"), listOf("Sunk Cost", "Money already spent that cannot be recovered", "NEVER consider sunk cost in forward decisions"))),
                s("Depreciation (Occasionally Tested)", tableHeaders = listOf("Method", "Description"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Straight-Line", "Same amount depreciated each year: (Cost − Salvage) ÷ Useful Life"), listOf("Accelerated (Double Declining Balance)", "More depreciation in early years; less later"), listOf("Sum-of-Years-Digits", "Another accelerated method; weights early years more heavily"))),
                s("Worked example — EVM — Step-by-Step Monthly Report Example", "Project: Open Banking API Integration | BAC = ₹60,00,000 | Duration: 12 months\n\nMonth 6 Status:\n\n• Planned: 50% complete by Month 6 → PV = ₹30,00,000\n\n• Actual work completed: 42% → EV = 0.42 × 60,00,000 = ₹25,20,000\n\n• Actual cost spent: ₹28,00,000 → AC = ₹28,00,000\n\nCalculate all EVM metrics:\n\nSV = EV − PV = 25,20,000 − 30,00,000 = −₹4,80,000 (behind schedule)\n\nCV = EV − AC = 25,20,000 − 28,00,000 = −₹2,80,000 (over budget)\n\nSPI = EV/PV = 25.2/30 = 0.84 (only 84% of planned work done)\n\nCPI = EV/AC = 25.2/28 = 0.90 (getting ₹0.90 of value per ₹1 spent)\n\nForecasts:\n\nEAC (typical — CPI continues) = BAC/CPI = 60,00,000/0.90 = ₹66,67,000\n\nETC = EAC − AC = 66,67,000 − 28,00,000 = ₹38,67,000 still to spend\n\nVAC = BAC − EAC = 60,00,000 − 66,67,000 = −₹6,67,000 forecast overrun\n\nTCPI (to BAC) = (60L−25.2L)/(60L−28L) = 34.8/32 = 1.09\n\nInterpretation for steering committee:\n\n\"The project is 8 weeks behind schedule and ₹2.8L over budget at the midpoint. If current efficiency continues, we forecast a ₹6.7L overrun. To meet the original budget, we need to improve cost efficiency by 9% from here — which is challenging. Recommended action: reduce overtime, optimize vendor costs in Phase 2, and re-baseline with a 6-week extension.\"", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Worked example — EAC Formula Selection — Decision Guide with Examples", "Read the exam question carefully for these CLUES:\n\nClue: \"Variances are typical / will continue\"\n\n→ EAC = BAC / CPI\n\nExample: CPI = 0.85. BAC = ₹100L. EAC = 100/0.85 = ₹117.6L\n\n(Current inefficiency has become the new normal — project continues at this rate)\n\nClue: \"Estimate was fundamentally flawed / start fresh\"\n\n→ EAC = AC + Bottom-up ETC\n\nExample: AC = ₹40L. Team re-estimates remaining work at ₹70L. EAC = 40+70 = ₹110L\n\n(Initial estimate was wrong — team knows better now)\n\nClue: \"Variance was atypical / one-time / will not recur\"\n\n→ EAC = AC + (BAC − EV)\n\nExample: A flood damaged equipment (one-time event). AC=₹40L, EV=₹35L, BAC=₹100L.\n\nEAC = 40 + (100−35) = 40+65 = ₹105L\n\n(Remaining work proceeds at PLANNED rate — only this one event caused the overrun)\n\nClue: \"Both cost and schedule efficiency affect the estimate\"\n\n→ EAC = AC + (BAC−EV)/(CPI×SPI)\n\n(Used when both time pressure and cost inefficiency compound each other)", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Worked example — NPV vs IRR vs Payback — Choosing a Project Example", "Three competing projects for the same ₹50L budget:\n\n | Metric | Project Alpha | Project Beta | Project Gamma\n\n | Investment | ₹50L | ₹50L | ₹50L\n\n | NPV | ₹28L ✅ | ₹18L | ₹22L\n\n | IRR | 22% | 28% ✅ | 25%\n\n | Payback | 3.5 years | 2.8 years | 1.9 years ✅\n\nPMI exam answer: Choose Project Alpha (highest NPV).\n\nWhy NPV wins: NPV accounts for time value of money AND total value created over the project life. Beta has better IRR but lower total value. Gamma pays back fastest but creates least total value.\n\nWhen payback matters: If the organisation has a cash flow constraint (\"we need the money back within 2 years\"), Gamma would be selected. The question will always specify which criterion to use.\n\nOpportunity cost example: If you choose Alpha, you give up Gamma. The opportunity cost of choosing Alpha is the value of Gamma (₹22L NPV). Opportunity cost is NEVER considered a sunk cost.", SectionKind.TIP),
            )
        ),
        Chapter(
            "quality",
            "✅ Quality Management (Governance Domain)",
            "Quality Philosophy",
            "Knowledge domains",
            listOf(
                s("Quality Philosophy", "• Prevention over inspection — build quality in from the start; it is always cheaper to prevent defects than to find and fix them\n• Customer satisfaction — meet or exceed stated AND unstated customer expectations. Fitness for use.\n• Continuous improvement (Kaizen) — always seek ways to improve processes and outputs; never stop improving\n• Cost of Quality: Money spent on achieving quality AND money lost from lack of quality — both must be managed\n• Quality is PLANNED in — it is not added at the end through testing and inspection alone"),
                s("Cost of Quality (COQ) — Know All 4 Categories", tableHeaders = listOf("Category", "Sub-type", "Examples", "PMI Preference"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Conformance", "Prevention", "Training, quality planning, process design, standards development, tool calibration", "✅ Preferred — cheapest in the long run"), listOf("Conformance", "Appraisal", "Testing, inspections, peer reviews, walkthroughs, quality audits", "Necessary — find defects before delivery"), listOf("Non-Conformance", "Internal Failure", "Rework, scrap, defect repair found BEFORE delivery to customer", "Expensive — avoid through prevention"), listOf("Non-Conformance", "External Failure", "Warranty claims, customer complaints, liability, recalls, lost business", "Most expensive — must be eliminated"))),
                s("Cost of Quality (COQ) — Know All 4 Categories", "Prevention costs are ALWAYS preferred. Internal failure is better than external failure. The cheapest time to fix a defect is when it is prevented. The most expensive is when the customer finds it.", SectionKind.KEY),
                s("Quality Processes", tableHeaders = listOf("Process", "Focus Area", "What It Does", "Key Output"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Plan Quality Management", "Planning", "Define quality standards and how to achieve/verify them", "Quality Management Plan, Quality Metrics, Test and Evaluation Documents"), listOf("Manage Quality", "Executing", "Translate quality plan into activities; perform quality audits; process analysis", "Quality Reports, Change Requests (process improvement)"), listOf("Control Quality", "M&C", "Inspect DELIVERABLES; verify they meet quality requirements; produce verified deliverables", "Verified Deliverables, Quality Control Measurements, Change Requests"))),
                s("7 Basic Quality Tools", "Cause & Effect (Fishbone / Ishikawa)"),
                s("Control Chart — Key Terms", tableHeaders = listOf("Term", "Definition", "Exam Point"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Mean", "Average value — centre line on control chart", "Baseline for comparison"), listOf("Control Limits", "±3 standard deviations from mean — set by the PROCESS", "Not set by customer — set by process capability"), listOf("Specification Limits", "Customer requirements — set by the CUSTOMER", "May be inside or outside control limits"), listOf("Rule of Seven", "7+ consecutive points on ONE SIDE of mean = process out of control", "Even if all within limits — investigate!"), listOf("Assignable Cause", "Variation from specific, identifiable cause — requires investigation", "Must be found and eliminated"), listOf("Common Cause", "Natural variation inherent in the process — acceptable if within limits", "If within limits — acceptable"))),
                s("Control Chart — Key Terms", "A process can be WITHIN control limits but still OUT OF CONTROL (Rule of Seven). Out of control = non-random pattern, NOT necessarily exceeding limits.", SectionKind.WARN),
                s("Quality Management Concepts — Deeper", tableHeaders = listOf("Concept", "Definition", "Exam Angle"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Quality vs. Grade", "Quality = degree to which characteristics fulfil requirements. Grade = category based on features.", "Low grade is acceptable (basic phone); low quality is NOT (broken phone)"), listOf("Precision vs. Accuracy", "Precision = consistency of measurements (tight cluster). Accuracy = closeness to true value.", "Can be precise but inaccurate (consistently wrong). Both needed."), listOf("Attribute Sampling", "Result conforms or does not (pass/fail)", "Used for go/no-go inspection"), listOf("Variable Sampling", "Result measured on a continuous scale (e.g., 4.7mm)", "Used for measuring degree of conformance"), listOf("Tolerance", "Specified range of acceptable results", "Set by requirements / customer"), listOf("Control Limits", "Boundaries of common variation in a stable process", "Set by the PROCESS (±3σ), not the customer"), listOf("Statistical Sampling", "Inspecting a subset rather than 100%", "Reduces cost of quality; sample size determined by confidence needed"))),
                s("Quality Gurus and Their Contributions", tableHeaders = listOf("Person", "Contribution", "Key Idea"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("W. Edwards Deming", "14 Points; PDCA cycle; 85% of quality problems are management's responsibility", "Plan-Do-Check-Act; drive out fear; continuous improvement"), listOf("Joseph Juran", "Quality Trilogy; \"fitness for use\"; Pareto principle applied to quality", "Planning, Control, Improvement; 80/20 rule"), listOf("Philip Crosby", "Zero Defects; \"quality is free\"; conformance to requirements", "Cost of quality — prevention is cheaper than failure"), listOf("Kaoru Ishikawa", "Fishbone diagram; quality circles; 7 basic quality tools", "Root cause analysis; employee involvement"), listOf("Genichi Taguchi", "Design of Experiments; loss function", "Quality should be designed in, not inspected in"))),
                s("PDCA / Continuous Improvement Cycle", tableHeaders = listOf("Step", "Activity"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Plan", "Identify the improvement opportunity; plan the change"), listOf("Do", "Implement the change on a small scale (pilot)"), listOf("Check", "Measure results; compare to expectations"), listOf("Act", "If successful, standardise; if not, learn and re-plan"))),
                s("Additional Quality Tools", tableHeaders = listOf("Tool", "Purpose"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Affinity Diagram", "Groups large numbers of ideas into natural categories"), listOf("Process Decision Program Chart (PDPC)", "Identifies what could go wrong in a plan and builds countermeasures"), listOf("Interrelationship Digraph", "Shows cause-effect relationships among multiple factors"), listOf("Tree Diagram", "Systematic breakdown of a topic into detailed components (like WBS for quality)"), listOf("Matrix Diagram", "Shows relationships between two or more sets of factors"), listOf("Prioritisation Matrix", "Ranks options against weighted criteria"), listOf("Design of Experiments (DOE)", "Statistical method to identify which variables most affect outcomes"), listOf("Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA)", "Identifies potential failures, their causes, effects, and prioritises by risk"))),
                s("Quality in Agile", "• Definition of Done: Team-agreed quality criteria every increment must meet\n• Test-Driven Development (TDD): Write tests first, then code to pass the tests\n• Continuous Integration: Code merged and tested frequently — catches defects early\n• Pair Programming: Two developers; one codes, one reviews in real time\n• Refactoring: Improving code structure without changing behaviour\n• Retrospectives: Regular process quality improvement\n• Technical Debt: Shortcuts taken that must be \"repaid\" later — should be tracked and managed"),
                s("Worked example — Cause and Effect (Fishbone) — Worked Example", "Defect: \"Mobile banking app crashes during peak hours (8–10 AM)\"\n\nDrawing the fishbone — 6Ms analysis:\n\nMan (People):\n\n→ Insufficient load testing by QA team\n\n→ Developers unaware of concurrent user limits\n\n→ No performance engineer on the project\n\nMachine (Technology/Equipment):\n\n→ Application server sized for 500 concurrent users; actual peak = 2,200\n\n→ Database connection pool exhausted under load\n\n→ No auto-scaling configured in cloud environment\n\nMethod (Process):\n\n→ Performance testing not included in Definition of Done\n\n→ No monitoring alerts for server capacity thresholds\n\n→ Deployment happens at 8 AM — peak usage time\n\nMaterial (Data/Inputs):\n\n→ Production data volume 10× larger than test data used\n\nMeasurement:\n\n→ Performance metrics not tracked during sprint reviews\n\n→ No SLA defined for response time under load\n\nEnvironment:\n\n→ Production uses shared infrastructure; other apps spike at same time\n\nRoot cause identified (via Pareto): 80% of crashes traced to database connection pool exhaustion (Machine) + no load testing in DoD (Method). Fix these two first.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Worked example — Control Chart — Reading in Real Life", "Process: Daily transaction processing error rate (%) over 30 days\n\nMean: 1.2% error rate\n\nUCL: 2.8% (Upper Control Limit = mean + 3σ)\n\nLCL: 0.0% (Lower Control Limit — cannot be negative for a rate)\n\nDay 1–14: Data points randomly scattered between 0.5% and 2.1% → Process IN control (common cause variation)\n\nDay 15–21: Seven consecutive points all ABOVE the mean (1.3%, 1.5%, 1.7%, 1.8%, 1.9%, 2.0%, 2.1%) — all still within UCL\n\n→ RULE OF SEVEN violated → Process OUT of control even though no limit was breached.\n\n→ Investigation reveals: a new team joined Day 15 and has different data entry training.\n\n→ Action: additional training; process standardisation.\n\nDay 22: Error rate jumps to 3.4% — ABOVE UCL\n\n→ Out of control — assignable cause. Investigation: a system update introduced a bug.\n\n→ Action: rollback the update; raise a defect repair change request.\n\nKey insight: The Rule of Seven caught the training issue BEFORE it became a limit violation. This is why you watch for PATTERNS, not just limit breaches.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Worked example — Cost of Quality — Real Project Breakdown", "Project: Insurance Claims Processing System (₹2 crore project)\n\nPREVENTION COSTS (invested to prevent defects):\n\n• Requirements workshop with business analysts: ₹1,20,000\n\n• Automated test suite setup: ₹2,50,000\n\n• Code review process (2h per PR): ₹3,20,000\n\n• Developer training on secure coding: ₹80,000\n\nTotal Prevention: ₹7,70,000 (3.85% of budget)\n\nAPPRAISAL COSTS (finding defects before delivery):\n\n• QA testing team: ₹4,00,000\n\n• UAT facilitation: ₹1,50,000\n\n• Performance testing: ₹1,20,000\n\nTotal Appraisal: ₹6,70,000 (3.35% of budget)\n\nINTERNAL FAILURE COSTS (fixing defects before delivery):\n\n• Rework on 47 defects found in testing: ₹3,80,000\n\n• Regression testing after fixes: ₹1,20,000\n\nTotal Internal Failure: ₹5,00,000 (2.5% of budget)\n\nEXTERNAL FAILURE COSTS (defects found in production):\n\n• 3 production bugs found post-launch; customer escalations: ₹2,50,000\n\n• Emergency hotfix deployment: ₹80,000\n\n• Regulatory fine for data error: ₹5,00,000\n\nTotal External Failure: ₹8,30,000 (4.15% of budget)\n\nTotal COQ: ₹27,70,000 (13.85% of budget)\n\nThe lesson: External failure cost (₹8.3L) is more than prevention + appraisal combined would have cost if the regulatory test had been included. Every ₹1 spent on prevention saves ₹3–5 in failure costs.", SectionKind.TIP),
            )
        ),
        Chapter(
            "risk",
            "⚠️ Risk Management",
            "Risk Management Process Flow",
            "Knowledge domains",
            listOf(
                s("Risk Management Process Flow", "Plan Risk Management\n\nIdentify Risks\n\nQualitative Analysis\n\nQuantitative Analysis (if needed)\n\nPlan Responses\n\nImplement Responses\n\nMonitor Risks"),
                s("Risk Management Process Flow", "Risk management is CONTINUOUS throughout the project — not a one-time activity. New risks emerge during execution and must be captured, analyzed, and responded to.", SectionKind.KEY),
                s("Qualitative vs. Quantitative Risk Analysis", tableHeaders = listOf("Qualitative Analysis", "Quantitative Analysis"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Purpose", "PRIORITISE risks by relative probability and impact — which risks matter most?", "NUMERICALLY model the effect of risks on project objectives"), listOf("When Done", "ALL projects — done first, before quantitative", "Only for LARGE, COMPLEX, HIGH-STAKES projects — not always done"), listOf("Method", "Probability × Impact Matrix (P×I); Risk Urgency; Risk Categorisation", "Monte Carlo Simulation; Decision Trees; Sensitivity Analysis (Tornado Chart)"), listOf("Accuracy", "Relative (High/Medium/Low)", "Specific numbers (probability of finishing by date, cost at risk)"), listOf("Output", "Prioritised risk list — which risks to plan responses for", "Statistical range of possible outcomes; contingency reserve amount"))),
                s("Qualitative vs. Quantitative Risk Analysis", "Expected Monetary Value (EMV)"),
                s("Risk Response Strategies — Threats AND Opportunities", tableHeaders = listOf("Strategy", "For THREATS (Negative Risks)", "For OPPORTUNITIES (Positive Risks)"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Escalate", "Risk is beyond PM's authority — notify and involve management", "Opportunity beyond PM's scope — notify management to act"), listOf("Avoid / Exploit", "ELIMINATE the cause of the threat entirely — change plan to remove it", "ENSURE the opportunity definitely occurs — take definitive action to guarantee it"), listOf("Transfer / Share", "Shift financial or operational impact to a THIRD PARTY (insurance, outsourcing)", "PARTNER with another entity to share the benefit and the effort"), listOf("Mitigate / Enhance", "Reduce PROBABILITY or IMPACT (or both) of the threat", "Increase PROBABILITY or IMPACT (or both) of the opportunity"), listOf("Accept — Active", "Create a CONTINGENCY PLAN that executes if the risk occurs", "Create a PLAN TO CAPITALISE if the opportunity arises"), listOf("Accept — Passive", "Acknowledge but take NO action — monitor only", "Acknowledge but take NO action — monitor only"))),
                s("Key Risk Terms", tableHeaders = listOf("Term", "Definition"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Risk Appetite", "The level of uncertainty an organisation is WILLING to accept in pursuit of its objectives — documented in the Risk Management Plan"), listOf("Risk Tolerance", "The SPECIFIC AMOUNT of risk a stakeholder or organisation is willing to accept — more granular than appetite"), listOf("Risk Threshold", "The TRIGGER POINT for action — below threshold: can accept; above: must respond with a defined strategy"), listOf("Secondary Risk", "A NEW risk that emerges AS A DIRECT RESULT of implementing a risk response strategy"), listOf("Residual Risk", "Risk that REMAINS after risk responses have been fully implemented — must be documented and monitored"), listOf("Workaround", "UNPLANNED response to a risk event — used when no contingency plan exists for that risk"), listOf("Fallback Plan", "BACKUP response used when the PRIMARY risk response proves inadequate or fails"), listOf("Risk Register", "Documents ALL identified risks, their characteristics, analysis results, and planned responses"), listOf("Risk Report", "Provides summary info on individual risks and overall project risk — separate from the register"), listOf("Tornado Chart", "Horizontal bar chart showing which risks have the greatest SENSITIVITY IMPACT on project objectives — widest bars = most influential risks"))),
                s("Monitor Risks — Key Inputs and Outputs (PMBOK 8 ITTO)", "Inputs\n • Project management plan (risk management plan)\n• Issue log, Lessons learned register\n• Risk register, Risk report\n• Work performance data and reports"),
                s("Outputs", "• Work performance information\n• Change requests\n• Project management plan updates\n• Project document updates: Assumption log, Issue log, Lessons learned register, Risk register, Risk report"),
                s("Risk Identification Techniques", tableHeaders = listOf("Technique", "Description", "Best For"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Brainstorming", "Team generates risk ideas freely", "Initial comprehensive identification"), listOf("Checklists", "Based on historical info and lessons learned", "Common risks in similar projects"), listOf("Interviews", "Experienced stakeholders and SMEs share risks", "Deep expertise; sensitive topics"), listOf("Root Cause Analysis", "Identify underlying causes of risks", "Grouping risks by cause"), listOf("SWOT Analysis", "Strengths, Weaknesses (internal); Opportunities, Threats (external)", "Both positive and negative risks"), listOf("Assumption & Constraint Analysis", "Test validity of assumptions — each false assumption is a risk", "Project planning; uncertain premises"), listOf("Document Analysis", "Review plans, contracts, requirements for inconsistencies", "Finding gaps and conflicts"), listOf("Prompt Lists", "Predetermined categories to stimulate thinking (PESTLE, TECOP, VUCA)", "Ensuring coverage of all risk categories"), listOf("Delphi Technique", "Anonymous expert consensus over rounds", "Avoiding bias and groupthink"))),
                s("Prompt Lists — PESTLE and TECOP", "PESTLE (External)\n • Political\n• Economic\n• Social\n• Technological\n• Legal\n• Environmental"),
                s("TECOP (Project)", "• Technical\n• Environmental\n• Commercial\n• Operational\n• Political"),
                s("Risk Breakdown Structure (RBS)", "Hierarchical representation of risks by category — like a WBS for risks. Typical top-level categories:"),
                s("Risk Breakdown Structure (RBS)", tableHeaders = listOf("Category", "Examples"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Technical", "Requirements, technology, complexity, interfaces, performance, quality"), listOf("External", "Subcontractors, regulatory, market, customer, weather"), listOf("Organisational", "Dependencies, resources, funding, prioritisation"), listOf("Project Management", "Estimating, planning, controlling, communication"))),
                s("Worked example — Probability and Impact Matrix — Worked Example", "Risk Score = Probability × Impact\n\nScale: Probability 1–5 (1=very low, 5=very high). Impact 1–5 (1=negligible, 5=catastrophic).\n\nRisk A: P=4, I=5 → Score = 20 (HIGH — must respond)\n\nRisk B: P=2, I=3 → Score = 6 (MEDIUM — monitor)\n\nRisk C: P=5, I=1 → Score = 5 (LOW — accept)\n\nRisk D: P=1, I=5 → Score = 5 (LOW score BUT high impact — consider a contingency plan)\n\nKey insight: Risk D has the same score as Risk C, but a catastrophic impact (even at low probability) often warrants a contingency plan. Do not rely purely on the score — use judgment.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Worked example — Decision Tree Analysis — Worked Example", "Scenario: Build a new facility or upgrade the existing one?\n\nOption 1: Build New — Cost \$200K\n\n  60% chance of high demand → revenue \$500K\n\n  40% chance of low demand → revenue \$200K\n\n  EMV = (0.6 × 500K) + (0.4 × 200K) − 200K = 300K + 80K − 200K = +\$180K\n\nOption 2: Upgrade Existing — Cost \$80K\n\n  60% chance of high demand → revenue \$300K\n\n  40% chance of low demand → revenue \$150K\n\n  EMV = (0.6 × 300K) + (0.4 × 150K) − 80K = 180K + 60K − 80K = +\$160K\n\nDecision: Build New has higher EMV (+\$180K vs +\$160K). Choose Build New.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Monte Carlo Simulation", "• Runs thousands of iterations using random values from probability distributions for each activity\n• Output: probability distribution of project completion date or cost (e.g., \"80% confident of finishing by June 15\")\n• Reveals which activities are most likely to end up on the critical path (criticality index)\n• Used in quantitative risk analysis for large/complex projects\n• Result is an S-curve showing cumulative probability"),
                s("Risk Management in Agile", tableHeaders = listOf("Practice", "How It Manages Risk"), tableRows = listOf(listOf("Short iterations", "Frequent delivery reduces the risk of building the wrong thing"), listOf("Sprint reviews", "Regular stakeholder feedback catches problems early"), listOf("Backlog prioritisation", "Highest-risk, highest-value items done first (risk-adjusted backlog)"), listOf("Spikes", "Time-boxed research to reduce technical uncertainty before committing"), listOf("Retrospectives", "Process risks identified and addressed each sprint"), listOf("Daily Scrum", "Impediments surfaced within 24 hours"), listOf("Risk burndown chart", "Tracks total risk exposure over sprints — should trend downward"))),
                s("Worked example — Risk Register — Complete Example", "Project: Core Banking System Migration (Bank of Maharashtra equivalent)\n\n | ID | Risk Description | Prob | Impact(₹L) | EMV | Strategy | Response | Owner\n\n | R-01 | Vendor delivers core module 4 weeks late | 35% | ₹40L | ₹14L | Mitigate | Weekly milestone reviews; pre-qualify backup vendor | PM\n\n | R-02 | RBI issues new API standard mid-project | 20% | ₹25L | ₹5L | Accept (active) | Contingency plan: modular API layer designed for easy reconfiguration | Arch Lead\n\n | R-03 | Key developer resigns during critical phase | 25% | ₹30L | ₹7.5L | Mitigate | Cross-training; all code documented; 2nd developer shadowing | Tech Lead\n\n | R-04 | Data migration reveals legacy data quality issues | 60% | ₹20L | ₹12L | Mitigate | Pre-migration data audit; data cleansing sprint added to plan | DBA\n\n | R-05 (Opportunity) | Early completion could win Year-end recognition | 30% | +₹15L | +₹4.5L | Enhance | Buffer tasks identified to pull forward if main tasks complete early | PM\n\nTotal contingency reserve needed: Sum of threat EMVs = 14+5+7.5+12 = ₹38.5L\n\nNet expected value (threats minus opportunities): 38.5−4.5 = ₹34L\n\nContingency reserve set at: ₹35L (reflects 80th percentile confidence)", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Worked example — Decision Tree — Make or Buy Analysis", "Decision: Should we build the payment reconciliation module in-house or buy a vendor solution?\n\nOption A: Build In-House — Cost: ₹25L\n\n• 70% chance project team can deliver on time → Revenue benefit: ₹80L → Net: ₹80L−₹25L=₹55L\n\n• 30% chance of delays (6 months) → Revenue benefit delayed: ₹50L → Net: ₹50L−₹25L=₹25L\n\nEMV(Build) = (0.70×₹55L) + (0.30×₹25L) = ₹38.5L + ₹7.5L = ₹46L\n\nOption B: Buy Vendor Solution — Cost: ₹18L\n\n• 90% chance vendor delivers as promised → Benefit: ₹75L → Net: ₹75L−₹18L=₹57L\n\n• 10% chance vendor delays (integration issues) → Benefit: ₹55L → Net: ₹55L−₹18L=₹37L\n\nEMV(Buy) = (0.90×₹57L) + (0.10×₹37L) = ₹51.3L + ₹3.7L = ₹55L\n\nDecision: Buy the vendor solution — higher EMV (₹55L vs ₹46L).\n\nAdditional considerations (beyond EMV):\n\n• Does building give us a strategic asset (IP, capability)? → May favour Build\n\n• Do we have the team capacity to build? → May favour Buy\n\n• Is the vendor financially stable? → Risk of Buy option\n\n• EMV is a decision TOOL — human judgment still applies.", SectionKind.TIP),
                s("Worked example — Risk Response — Secondary Risk Example", "Original Risk (R-03): Key developer may resign.\n\nResponse: Cross-train a second developer on the same module.\n\nSecondary Risk created by the response:\n\n\"Cross-training takes the primary developer 4 hours/week away from delivery tasks, potentially reducing sprint velocity by 10–15%.\"\n\nTreatment of secondary risk:\n\nAdd to Risk Register as R-03a:\n\n• Probability: 80% (very likely cross-training reduces velocity)\n\n• Impact: −1 to 2 story points per sprint = minor\n\n• Strategy: Accept (cost of cross-training is justified by mitigation of R-03)\n\n• Monitor: Track velocity weekly; if drop exceeds 15%, adjust cross-training schedule\n\nResidual Risk (what remains of R-03 after the response):\n\nEven with cross-training, if the developer leaves on Day 1 of a new phase, the second developer will need 2 weeks to get up to speed. This residual risk is accepted and covered in the contingency reserve.", SectionKind.TIP),
            )
        ),
        Chapter(
